1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">Spherical CNNs generalize convolutional networks to
signals living on the sphere,</font>

2
00:00:05,874 --> 00:00:09,908
<font color="#FFFFFF">which makes them a natural fit for molecules and
weather data.</font>

3
00:00:09,988 --> 00:00:14,420
<font color="#FFFFFF">But until now they were stuck at low resolutions
and shallow depths,</font>

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,334
<font color="#FFFFFF">so they never really competed on large real-world
problems.</font>

5
00:00:18,414 --> 00:00:22,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, Scaling Spherical CNNs from Google
Research and MIT,</font>

6
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:26,628
<font color="#FFFFFF">shows how to scale these models by a full order of
magnitude.</font>

7
00:00:26,708 --> 00:00:30,144
<font color="#FFFFFF">With new activations, normalization, residual blocks,</font>

8
00:00:30,224 --> 00:00:32,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a TPU-optimized implementation,</font>

9
00:00:32,546 --> 00:00:38,305
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors reach state of the art on the QM9
molecular benchmark and become competitive</font>

10
00:00:38,385 --> 00:00:40,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">on several weather forecasting tasks.</font>

11
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:45,958
<font color="#FFFFFF">Spherical CNNs replace the plane with the sphere as
the domain of the signal,</font>

12
00:00:46,038 --> 00:00:50,156
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exactly right for data like molecules and
the atmosphere.</font>

13
00:00:50,236 --> 00:00:54,099
<font color="#FFFFFF">The catch is that their core operation, spherical
convolution,</font>

14
00:00:54,179 --> 00:00:56,643
<font color="#FFFFFF">is most accurate in the spectral domain,</font>

15
00:00:56,723 --> 00:01:00,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that is far more expensive than an ordinary
planar convolution.</font>

16
00:01:00,985 --> 00:01:04,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because of this cost, spherical CNNs had been
limited to small,</font>

17
00:01:04,992 --> 00:01:08,156
<font color="#FFFFFF">low-resolution problems with modest model capacity.</font>

18
00:01:08,236 --> 00:01:14,007
<font color="#FFFFFF">There simply was no large-scale spherical
architecture analogous to the deep planar networks</font>

19
00:01:14,087 --> 00:01:16,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">that power modern computer vision.</font>

20
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:23,699
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two scientific problems motivate this work:
predicting molecular properties and forecasting the weather.</font>

21
00:01:23,779 --> 00:01:27,522
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both are intrinsically spherical and tied to rotations.</font>

22
00:01:27,602 --> 00:01:32,039
<font color="#FFFFFF">A molecule's properties don't change when you
rotate it in space,</font>

23
00:01:32,119 --> 00:01:36,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the Earth's atmosphere is naturally a signal on
a sphere.</font>

24
00:01:36,359 --> 00:01:40,589
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rotation-equivariant spherical CNNs should be a
perfect match.</font>

25
00:01:40,669 --> 00:01:43,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">But the standard benchmarks are large.</font>

26
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:46,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">QM9 has one hundred thirty four thousand molecules,</font>

27
00:01:46,855 --> 00:01:52,683
<font color="#FFFFFF">over eighteen times bigger than the tiny QM7 set
earlier spherical CNNs could handle,</font>

28
00:01:52,763 --> 00:01:56,089
<font color="#FFFFFF">and weather grids demand high spatial resolution.</font>

29
00:01:56,169 --> 00:01:58,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">To compete, these models had to scale.</font>

30
00:01:59,110 --> 00:02:02,685
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors contribute a systematic recipe for
scaling spherical CNNs by an order of magnitude.</font>

31
00:02:02,765 --> 00:02:06,339
<font color="#FFFFFF">It has three parts.</font>

32
00:02:06,419 --> 00:02:09,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

33
00:02:10,074 --> 00:02:13,648
<font color="#FFFFFF">an efficient implementation of spin-weighted
spherical harmonic transforms in JAX,</font>

34
00:02:13,728 --> 00:02:17,303
<font color="#FFFFFF">tuned to run fast and distributed on TPUs.</font>

35
00:02:17,383 --> 00:02:20,957
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

36
00:02:21,037 --> 00:02:24,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">new general-purpose layers and activations that
improve both expressivity and efficiency.</font>

37
00:02:24,692 --> 00:02:28,266
<font color="#FFFFFF">And third,</font>

38
00:02:28,346 --> 00:02:31,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">application-specific input representations designed
for molecules and for weather data.</font>

39
00:02:32,001 --> 00:02:35,575
<font color="#FFFFFF">A key finding is that naive scaling, just adding
depth and width, is not enough;</font>

40
00:02:35,655 --> 00:02:39,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">the core components themselves had to be redesigned.</font>

41
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:42,722
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method builds on spin-weighted spherical CNNs.</font>

42
00:02:42,802 --> 00:02:47,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its centerpiece is a set of new components that all
live in the spectral domain.</font>

43
00:02:47,908 --> 00:02:53,446
<font color="#FFFFFF">A phase collapse nonlinearity takes the modulus of
the features to collapse their phase,</font>

44
00:02:53,526 --> 00:02:58,808
<font color="#FFFFFF">which restores rotation invariance while losing no
information in the nonzero spins.</font>

45
00:02:58,888 --> 00:03:03,403
<font color="#FFFFFF">Batch normalization and pooling are also moved into
the spectral domain,</font>

46
00:03:03,483 --> 00:03:08,893
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the residual block adds its skip connection
directly between Fourier coefficients.</font>

47
00:03:08,973 --> 00:03:10,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the implementation side,</font>

48
00:03:10,697 --> 00:03:17,702
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors compute the Fourier transforms as dense
matrix multiplications rather than fast Fourier transforms,</font>

49
00:03:17,782 --> 00:03:23,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">because on TPUs matrix multiplies are extremely
fast while memory reshuffling is the bottleneck.</font>

50
00:03:24,210 --> 00:03:28,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments span two very different domains.</font>

51
00:03:28,587 --> 00:03:32,885
<font color="#FFFFFF">For molecules, the benchmark is QM9, with one
hundred thirty four thousand molecules,</font>

52
00:03:32,965 --> 00:03:37,262
<font color="#FFFFFF">up to twenty nine atoms each, and twelve regression
targets covering energetic,</font>

53
00:03:37,342 --> 00:03:41,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">electronic, and thermodynamic properties.</font>

54
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:46,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">For weather,</font>

55
00:03:46,097 --> 00:03:50,395
<font color="#FFFFFF">the models are trained on ERA5 reanalysis data
through the WeatherBench benchmark,</font>

56
00:03:50,475 --> 00:03:54,772
<font color="#FFFFFF">forecasting quantities like geopotential height and
temperature at three and five day horizons,</font>

57
00:03:54,852 --> 00:03:59,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus longer tasks reaching out to twenty eight days
and iterative high-resolution forecasting.</font>

58
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:03,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are strong on both fronts.</font>

59
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:08,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">On QM9, the scaled spherical CNN reaches state of the art,</font>

60
00:04:08,290 --> 00:04:12,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">beating the previously dominant graph neural
networks and transformers on eight of twelve</font>

61
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:16,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">targets under the first data split and nine of
twelve under the second.</font>

62
00:04:17,050 --> 00:04:21,350
<font color="#FFFFFF">On weather,</font>

63
00:04:21,430 --> 00:04:25,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">it outperforms the WeatherBench baseline on every
metric in the simpler two-predictor setting,</font>

64
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:30,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it even beats models that were pre-trained on
large amounts of simulated data on several temperature metrics.</font>

65
00:04:30,190 --> 00:04:34,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is the first demonstration that spherical CNNs
are viable neural weather models.</font>

66
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:38,224
<font color="#FFFFFF">A careful ablation isolates the effect of each change.</font>

67
00:04:38,304 --> 00:04:40,578
<font color="#FFFFFF">Starting from the JAX implementation,</font>

68
00:04:40,658 --> 00:04:44,267
<font color="#FFFFFF">the phase collapse activation cuts error by eight percent,</font>

69
00:04:44,347 --> 00:04:48,591
<font color="#FFFFFF">spectral batch normalization trims a further one
and a half percent,</font>

70
00:04:48,671 --> 00:04:52,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the efficient residual block another two and a
half percent,</font>

71
00:04:52,742 --> 00:04:54,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">all while improving speed.</font>

72
00:04:54,395 --> 00:04:59,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">A separate comparison confirms that the phase
collapse activation, spectral pooling,</font>

73
00:04:59,738 --> 00:05:05,827
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the new spherical molecule representation each
beat the prior alternatives from earlier work,</font>

74
00:05:05,907 --> 00:05:12,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">together driving the QM9 enthalpy error down to
about fifteen point two five milli electron volts.</font>

75
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:15,547
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here are the numbers that summarize the impact.</font>

76
00:05:15,627 --> 00:05:21,582
<font color="#FFFFFF">The models scale by one full order of magnitude in
both operations and feature resolution</font>

77
00:05:21,662 --> 00:05:23,819
<font color="#FFFFFF">compared to prior spherical CNNs.</font>

78
00:05:23,899 --> 00:05:31,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">They set state of the art on eight of twelve QM9
targets in the first split and nine of twelve in the second.</font>

79
00:05:31,290 --> 00:05:36,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">The new JAX implementation is about three times
faster than the original,</font>

80
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:42,059
<font color="#FFFFFF">and running distributed across thirty two TPUs
speeds it up by a hundred times or more.</font>

81
00:05:42,139 --> 00:05:47,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the phase collapse nonlinearity alone reduces
QM9 error by eight percent.</font>

82
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:52,002
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is that spherical CNNs were never
fundamentally limited,</font>

83
00:05:52,082 --> 00:05:53,861
<font color="#FFFFFF">they were just poorly scaled.</font>

84
00:05:53,941 --> 00:05:58,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">With a redesign of the nonlinearity, normalization,
and residual block,</font>

85
00:05:58,492 --> 00:06:01,744
<font color="#FFFFFF">plus an implementation tuned to modern accelerators,</font>

86
00:06:01,824 --> 00:06:04,564
<font color="#FFFFFF">these models finally scale to real problems,</font>

87
00:06:04,644 --> 00:06:11,999
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaching state of the art on molecular property
prediction and becoming genuinely competitive neural weather models.</font>

88
00:06:12,079 --> 00:06:18,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors release their JAX implementation as a
platform for further research on spherical data.</font>
